I tried asking myself what I'd learned after 40, and it turned out to be a lot of the most important stuff I know. At least 90% of what I know about startups, because I was 40 when we started YC. Plus everything I know about parenting and most of what I know about writing.

@AJ_1121 So many things! Most of my essays are about something I realized recently, often while writing it. And if I hadn't realized something till recently, that means I was at least implicitly wrong about it before.

@lorenzo_madeit That's why I said 90% rather than 100%. But think how much I've learned about startups from 20 years of YC. Even at the end of running Viaweb I was completely clueless by my current standards.

@KrittanawongMD It's a common number you hear in statements of the form "People don't learn much after &lt;age&gt;."

@JCNVV90 Money itself doesn't bring you much happiness. Things you buy with it can. But the returns are nothing like linear. 10x as much money might make you 10% happier in the best case.

I've been writing de facto essays in notebooks since my early 20s. But what triggered publishing that sort of thing was when something I wrote about Lisp ended up on Slashdot.

@bobthewizard23 Those years go fast and you can never get them back. So spend as much time with your kids as you can. No one ever regrets how much time they spent with their kids.

@lunehire If I could start over I'd follow curiosity instead of chasing prestige. I gradually learned to do this, but only after being fooled multiple times.

